---
title: Defining materials
description: Declare immutable shader and resource contracts with defineMaterial.
---

`defineMaterial(...)` is the public boundary between application input and the renderer. It validates the fragment and resource declarations, clones mutable inputs that require snapshots, and freezes the resulting `FragMaterial`.

## Input

| Field | Required | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fragment` | Yes | WGSL containing `fn frag(uv: vec2f) -> vec4f` |
| `uniforms` | No | Initial values and uniform types |
| `textures` | No | Sampled or storage texture declarations |
| `defines` | No | Compile-time WGSL constants |
| `includes` | No | Named source chunks expanded by `#include` |
| `storageBuffers` | No | Compute-writable and fragment-readable buffers |

```ts
const material = defineMaterial({
	fragment,
	uniforms: { uTime: 0, uColor: [0.2, 0.8, 1] },
	textures: { uImage: { colorSpace: 'srgb' } },
	defines: { ENABLE_GLOW: true }
});
```

## Validation

The fragment must contain exactly the supported entrypoint signature:

```wgsl
fn frag(uv: vec2f) -> vec4f
```

Uniform, texture, define, include, and storage-buffer names must match `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`. Definitions are validated before renderer creation, including uniform shapes, define values, storage-buffer alignment and access, and storage-texture requirements.

## Immutability

The material and its top-level declaration maps are frozen. Storage definitions are frozen, and typed-array `initialData` is cloned into the snapshot. `Float32Array` matrix defaults are also cloned. Create a new material to change declarations; use frame APIs to change runtime values.

Do not construct `FragMaterial` objects manually. `resolveMaterial(...)` accepts materials created by `defineMaterial(...)` and rejects unsupported handcrafted objects.

## Signature

Resolution preprocesses includes and defines, sorts layout-dependent resource keys, and builds a deterministic signature from:

- final fragment WGSL;
- uniform names and types;
- texture keys and normalized texture configuration;
- storage-buffer declarations and `initialData` contents;
- storage texture keys.

Pure uniform values are not part of the signature. Texture definition values are; changing a texture source through `setTexture(...)` does not change the material declaration.

See [Material API](/docs/api-material-reference) for types, [Uniforms](/docs/uniforms) and [Textures](/docs/textures) for resource contracts, and [Shader Includes & Defines](/docs/shader-includes-and-defines) for preprocessing.
